ifferent and this week  of course your leader Tim Farron was  forced to clarify that he doesn't think  that being gay is a sin he's also  wrestled with the laws over abortion as  a committed Christian if you are trying  to pitch yourself trying to win back  those votes of their kind of liberals if  you like who perhaps feel they don't  have a natural home in labour all the  conservatives at the moment it's Tim  power and really the man to do it yes  and we are a Liberal Party in that wider  social sense and he's voting record is  as of a liberal MP and then the problem  he has as an individual and it's true of  a lot of evangelical Christians and  Roman Catholics is that their religious  faith has a certain approach these  problems but there are also public  figures who have to represent their  constituencies which are much more  diverse and I think Tim accepts and I  think you know many highly motivated  Christians accept that when they're in  public life they've got to reflect the  people that they represent and he's done  so and he's done so in a very liberal  way there's nothing to detract from our  broader message about being a socially  liberal party so he's effectively you  might say out of step with what no he  has a personal faith and I respect  people  who have a personal face he's struggling  with his conscience as a lot of  Christians and indeed Muslims and others  our that he is when it's come to the  crunch to the vote voting for gay  marriage voting on these issues he has  voted consistently with a in a liberal  way along with the rest of our party and  you believe that's because he wants to  put the views of the people he's  representing above his own personal  views yes and it's difficult because if  you are a true you know if you have real  faith that is not an easy thing to do  and I think one has to respect people  who are grappling with their faith in  the way that he is now  we're talking a lot about the election  of course and as you'd expect 